        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Neoliberalism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1938 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek">Friedrich Hayek</a> and Ludwig von Mises, who held that the state's only legitimate role was to abolish barriers to market entry; Walter Lippmann, Alexander Rüstow and Wilhelm Röpke, who favoured strong state supervision of the economy; Walter Eucken and Alfred Müller-Armack of the German social market economy; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-friedman">Milton Friedman</a>, founder of monetarism; George Stigler and James M. Buchanan</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val">Walter Lippmann’s An Inquiry into the Principles of the Good Society; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-friedman">Milton Friedman</a>’s essay “Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">Liberalism</a> and its Prospects” (1951) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">Capitalism</a> and Freedom; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek">Friedrich Hayek</a>’s The Road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">Serfdom</a>; Charles Peters’s “A Neoliberal’s Manifesto” (1983); John Williamson’s ten rules of the Washington Consensus (1989)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">the priority of the price mechanism; free enterprise and competition; privatisation; deregulation; free trade; monetarism and price stability; lower taxes and reduced government spending; economic and political freedom as linked</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Neoliberal policies are associated with governments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, the United Kingdom, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and many others from the 1970s and 1980s, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">IMF</a> and World Bank; the term is used mainly by critics and is largely absent from economics journals, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a> Institute changed its label from libertarian to neoliberal in 2016</span></div>
