    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The term was chosen in 1938 at the Walter Lippmann Colloquium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where its founders agreed that laissez-faire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> had failed but disagreed sharply over how strong the state should be.</li><li>Its policies were widely implemented only after the economic crises of the 1970s, first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-pinochet">Augusto Pinochet</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a>, then under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a>, and then abroad through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">IMF</a> and World Bank loan conditions.</li><li>Scholars dispute what the word means: it is often used pejoratively and rarely by proponents of free-market policies, and some scholars call it an essentially contested concept.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>At the 1938 Colloquium that named neoliberalism, Ludwig von Mises denounced the ordoliberalism of the rival faction as “ordo-interventionism”, while Alexander Rüstow wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek">Hayek</a> and Mises were relics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> that caused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

