    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1898</span><span class="rf-text">The French economist Charles Gide uses the term in English to describe the beliefs of the Italian economist Maffeo Pantaleoni</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1938</span><span class="rf-text">The Walter Lippmann Colloquium meets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and chooses “neoliberalism” as the name of a new liberal project</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1947</span><span class="rf-text">The Mont Pelerin Society is founded, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek">Hayek</a>, Friedman, Popper, Stigler and Mises among its founding members</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1973</span><span class="rf-text">A coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> establishes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-pinochet">Augusto Pinochet</a>, which later adopts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chicago-boys">Chicago Boys</a>’ reforms</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1979</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a> becomes Conservative prime minister, a post she holds until 1990</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1981</span><span class="rf-text">Congress follows Reagan’s proposal and cuts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> federal income taxes across the board by 25%</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1984</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lange">David Lange</a> appoints <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-douglas">Roger Douglas</a> finance minister in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, beginning the reforms known as Rogernomics</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1989</span><span class="rf-text">John Williamson sets out the ten rules later called the Washington Consensus</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek.jpg" alt="Friedrich Hayek" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek">Friedrich Hayek</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_Hayek_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The original uploader was DickClarkMises at English Wikipedia., CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Neoliberalism originated among European liberal scholars during the 1930s, as a response to the perceived decline in popularity of classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>, which was seen as giving way to a social liberal desire to control markets. An early use of the term in English came in 1898 from the French economist Charles Gide, who applied it to the economic beliefs of the Italian economist Maffeo Pantaleoni; the term néo-libéralisme already existed in French.</p><p>In August 1938 twenty-five liberal intellectuals, among them Walter Lippmann, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek">Friedrich Hayek</a>, Ludwig von Mises, Wilhelm Röpke, Alexander Rüstow and Louis Rougier, met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> at the Walter Lippmann Colloquium, held to mark the French translation of Lippmann’s An Inquiry into the Principles of the Good Society. They called for a new liberal project and chose “neoliberalism”, one name among several floated, defining it as involving “the priority of the price mechanism, free enterprise, the system of competition, and a strong and impartial state”. Most agreed that laissez-faire liberalism had failed, but they disagreed deeply on the state’s role: a group around Rüstow and Lippmann advocated strong state supervision of the economy, while Mises and Hayek insisted that the only legitimate role for the state was to abolish barriers to market entry.</p><p>Divided and short of funds, the Colloquium was mostly ineffectual and was overwhelmed by the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. It nonetheless served as the first meeting of the nascent movement and as the precursor to the Mont Pelerin Society.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/milton-friedman"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/milton-friedman.jpg" alt="Milton Friedman" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/milton-friedman">Milton Friedman</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Milton_Friedman_(4x5_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bachrach Studios, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Neoliberal thinkers differed over what they proposed. In his early essay “Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">Liberalism</a> and Its Prospects”, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-friedman">Milton Friedman</a> wrote that neoliberalism would accept the nineteenth-century liberal emphasis on the individual but substitute for laissez-faire the goal of “the competitive order”, which requires limited state intervention to “police the system, establish conditions favorable to competition and prevent monopoly, provide a stable monetary framework, and relieve acute misery and distress”. By the 1970s neoliberal thought, Friedman’s included, focused almost exclusively on market liberalisation and opposed nearly all state interference in the economy.</p><p>Its policies centre on economic liberalisation: reductions to trade barriers, deregulation of industry, privatisation of state-owned enterprises, reductions in government spending and monetarism. Neoliberal theory contends that free markets encourage efficiency, growth and innovation, and state intervention is generally believed to worsen economic performance. Monetarism, formulated by Friedman, holds that excessive expansion of the money supply is inflationary and that monetary authorities should focus on price stability.</p><p>Many neoliberal thinkers hold that economic and political freedom are linked. Friedman argued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">Capitalism</a> and Freedom that economic freedom is a necessary condition for political freedom; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek">Hayek</a> wrote in The Road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">Serfdom</a> that “Economic control is not merely control of a sector of human life which can be separated from the rest; it is the control of the means for all our ends”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-erhard"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludwig-erhard.jpg" alt="Ludwig Erhard" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-erhard">Ludwig Erhard</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Einde_bezoek_bondskanselier_dr_Ludwig_Erhard_en_gaf_persconferentie_in_het_Haag,_Bestanddeelnr_916-1330.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eric Koch for Anefo, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Neoliberalism began accelerating in importance with the establishment of the Mont Pelerin Society in 1947. For decades its ideas stayed largely on the fringes of policy, confined to think-tanks and universities, with measured success only among the ordoliberals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. There the economists around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-erhard">Ludwig Erhard</a> drew on theories developed in the 1930s and 1940s, and Alfred Müller-Armack coined the phrase “social market economy”. In Germany neoliberalism at first meant both ordoliberalism and the social market economy, but the original term gradually disappeared as the latter fitted better with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wirtschaftswunder">Wirtschaftswunder</a> mentality of the 1950s and 1960s.</p><p>It was not until a succession of economic downturns and crises in the 1970s that neoliberal proposals were widely implemented. The Reagan administration and Thatcher government began neoliberal reforms in the early 1980s to counter the stagflation each country had experienced in the 1970s, and policies of this kind were then exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, the Asia-Pacific, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a> and World Bank encouraged reform in many developing countries by placing requirements on loans, a process known as structural adjustment, whose principles became known as the Washington Consensus.</p><p>A “second wave” followed in the 1990s: Bill Clinton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tony-blair">Tony Blair</a>, identifying with a moderate position known as the Third Way, embraced a neoliberal ideology. Some scholars say the rise of neoliberalism around the world began when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> dissolved and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> ended.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/salvador-allende"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salvador-allende.jpg" alt="Salvador Allende" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/salvador-allende">Salvador Allende</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salvador_Allende_Gossens-.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Biblioteca del Congreso Nacional de Chile, CC BY 3.0 cl</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> was among the earliest nations to adopt neoliberal reform. After a 1973 coup overthrew President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador-allende">Salvador Allende</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-pinochet">Augusto Pinochet</a>, known for its violent suppression of opposition, gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chicago-boys">Chicago Boys</a>, Chilean economists who had studied under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-friedman">Milton Friedman</a>, significant political influence. They carried out rapid privatisation of state enterprises, deregulation and reductions in trade barriers, and the central bank raised interest rates from 49.9% to 178%. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">Inflation</a> fell from over 600% in 1974 to below 50% by 1979, but inequality widened. After a severe recession in 1982 the economy grew quickly; Friedman called the period the “Miracle of Chile”, while some scholars call it a failure that led to extreme inequalities. Alejandro Foxley states that around 44% of Chilean families lived below the poverty line when Pinochet left office in 1990.</p><p>In the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a>’s governments from 1979 to 1990 reduced taxes, deregulated and privatised, and reduced the power of trade unions. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, Reagan’s administration cut taxes and deregulated finance, and the Clinton administration passed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-american-free-trade-agreement">NAFTA</a> and repealed the Glass–Steagall Act.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> the military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>’s 1976 programme froze salaries, and poverty rose from 9% in 1975 to 40% at the end of 1982. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberto-fujimori">Alberto Fujimori</a> adopted aggressive neoliberal policies after taking office in 1990, and at least 300,000 Peruvians were victims of forced sterilisation by his government in the 1990s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amartya-sen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amartya-sen.jpg" alt="Amartya Sen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amartya-sen">Amartya Sen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amartya_Sen_20071128_cologne_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elke Wetzig (Elya); derivative work by ChaudhryAzan, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Critics include economists Joseph Stiglitz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amartya-sen">Amartya Sen</a>, Ha-Joon Chang and Thomas Piketty, the linguist Noam Chomsky, the geographer David Harvey and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-francis">Pope Francis</a>. Harvey describes neoliberalism as a “class project” designed to increase the class power of economic elites; Wendy Brown asserts that its overriding objective is “the economization of all features of life”; Paul Krugman argues that the “laissez-faire absolutism” promoted by neoliberals fostered an intellectual climate in which faith in markets often trumps the evidence. Loïc Wacquant and Bernard Harcourt link mass incarceration in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to neoliberal policy, and Pope Francis called unfettered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> “a new tyranny”.</p><p>A 2016 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">IMF</a> paper found “much to cheer in the neoliberal agenda” but concluded that freedom of capital and fiscal consolidation resulted in increasing inequality, and that the benefits in growth were difficult to establish. Kristen Ghodsee argues that neoliberal capitalism left economic misery in the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a> and fuelled a resurgence of extremist nationalism.</p><p>Critics also come from the free-market side. Sheldon Richman argues that the IMF imposed a “corporatist-flavored ‘neoliberalism’”, and Mises accused the German neoliberals of “ordo-interventionism”. Will Hutton called the word “an unthinking leftist insult”, and Daniel Stedman Jones says it is too often a catch-all shorthand for the horrors associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/globalisation">globalisation</a> and financial crises.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The term entered common academic use in the 1980s in association with Pinochet’s reforms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, quickly acquired negative connotations, and by 1994 had entered global circulation. One study of 148 scholarly articles found it almost never defined. Scholars therefore describe it as an essentially contested concept; some, such as Gary Gerstle, hold that it remains a meaningful term, and Stephen Metcalf argues that the 2016 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">IMF</a> paper helps put to rest the idea that it is merely a slur. Gerstle writes that Reagan was the ideological architect of the neoliberal order and Clinton its key facilitator.</p><p>The Great Recession of 2008 gave rise to a surge of scholarship critical of neoliberalism. Political opposition from the late 1990s onward included the “pink tide” of leftist governments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, the yellow vests protests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 2018 and the Chilean protests of 2019–2021. During the 2021 Chilean general election Gabriel Boric promised to end the country’s neoliberal economic model, stating that “if Chile was the cradle of neoliberalism, it will also be its grave”. Adam Kotsko describes neoliberalism as a political theology that aspires to be a complete way of life.</p><p>Neoliberalism has been a significant factor in the proliferation of conservative and right-libertarian organisations, parties and think-tanks, which predominantly advocate it. The term remains largely absent from economics journals.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Neoliberalism is a free-market ideology named at a 1938 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> colloquium and spread by the Mont Pelerin Society. Its policies were widely applied from the 1970s, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> under Pinochet, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">IMF</a> and World Bank. Scholars dispute the term, which is often pejorative.</em></p>

